Changelog: 23-10-2026 04:53:35

Release 0.3.0.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 04:33:12

Added. The preview reads the camera through V4L2 itself (v4l2.py, the standard library's ioctl and mmap) instead of
Qt Multimedia, and Qt only turns its YUYV frames into pictures. Mode now offers every frame size at each of its rates
(25 modes on the owner's camera, where Qt listed 13): Qt reported one rate range per size and always streamed at its
fastest, so with Reduced bandwidth the side-by-side rates that fit, 2560x800 at 30 or 15 fps and 3840x1080 and
5148x1088 at 8, could not be chosen. A frame the driver could not complete (V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR, or short) is no
longer shown: Qt showed them, half of each frame flat red and green, at 2560x800 and 60 fps. When only damaged frames
come for 5 s the preview stops and says the mode needs more USB bandwidth; when nothing comes, that the camera sends
no picture in this mode; a camera another app holds is named as such. Measured on the owner's board (23-10-2026, every
mode for 3 s with Reduced bandwidth): every one-camera mode works except 1920x1080 at 15 fps, which sends nothing, as
do 3840x1080 and 5148x1088 at 15; 2560x800 at 60 and 3840x1080 and 5148x1088 at 30 arrive damaged; the rest works.
Qt pads its frame rows for some widths (5148 becomes 10304 bytes a row, not 10296), so a frame is copied row by row
then.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 04:22:12

Added. Show names the lens of each half of a side-by-side frame: "Left half: the lens on your left", "Right half: the
lens on your right", as you face the camera; the list's tooltip says so. Measured on the owner's board (23-10-2026):
with the lens on the left seen from behind covered, the right half of a 2560x800 frame went dark (mean luma 16
against 109).

Changelog: 23-10-2026 04:05:15

Release 0.2.0.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 04:02:12

Added. When no camera is found at all, the window says what to do if it is plugged in (in the picture area and the
state's tooltip), and so does the load service in its journal ("No camera found." with the same text, where it said
only that no camera waits for its firmware): after a restart of the board the camera stays silent, so unplug it and
plug it in again, or shut the board down and switch it on again. Found on the owner's board (23-10-2026): a warm
restart keeps the USB ports powered, the camera keeps its firmware but does not connect, and Linux cannot power-cycle
the ports; a replug and a cold start both bring it back. The picture area wraps its text.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 03:20:11

Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 23-10-2026 01:35:37

Added. First version. USB HD Cam makes the PS5 HD Camera (CFI-ZEY1) a webcam on Bazzite. The camera
has no flash: it starts as an OmniVision OV580 boot loader (USB ID 05a9:0580) and needs its firmware at every plug-in;
then it is a USB video camera (05a9:058c) that uvcvideo drives, so no kernel driver is needed. The app sends the
firmware as the PS4EYECam driver and PS Move API do (512-byte vendor control transfers, then the start request), over
usbfs with the standard library only. The firmware is Sony's and not in the suite: Download fetches the PS5 HD Camera's file
21.01-03.20.00.04 from prosperodev/hdcamera, pinned to a commit and checked by SHA-256, or Use a file takes the user's
own (the app knows a file by its SHA-256 and shows the check: the Firmware box green with the version for a known
file, orange for an own one, with the hash in the window; one known for the other camera is refused). Allow access adds a udev rule
(pkexec, once) that gives the logged-in user the boot loader (uaccess) and starts the user's load service when the
camera is plugged in (SYSTEMD_USER_WANTS); install.sh writes that service, which also runs at login for a camera
plugged in before, so nothing runs as root. Load it by itself (on by default) can be switched off; the window then
loads on Load firmware, and otherwise loads once per plug-in what the service could not. The header shows the model,
the port's USB speed (the video needs USB 3; a slower port is flagged), the firmware and the video node. The preview
(Qt Multimedia) offers every mode the camera reports, one camera's picture or both side by side, shows a side-by-side
frame whole or either half, and saves what it shows as a PNG; it holds the camera only while it runs. Picture
settings beside it offers every control the camera reports (V4L2 control ioctls with the standard library; on the
owner's board brightness, contrast, saturation, hue, gain, sharpness, automatic white balance and its
temperature, power line frequency, and the exposure: Auto or Shutter Priority), grays one that another decides,
and has Defaults; the values the user changed are kept and set again each time the camera is ready, by the window
and by the load service, as the camera forgets them unplugged. Measured on the owner's board (23-10-2026, 1280x800,
a well-lit room): mean luma 48 in the camera's default Shutter Priority (24 % near black), 111 in Auto Mode, 136
with gain 8, 166 with brightness 8; Shutter Priority with brightness 8 gives 112 with less clipped white; gain does
nothing in Shutter Priority, and the exposure time stays at 1000 whatever is set (its whole row shows grayed, as
does any control the camera does not let be changed). Auto Mode is the app's default (the owner's choice, 23-10-2026),
set at every plug-in unless the user chose otherwise; Defaults keeps it. The camera's one vendor control (extension
unit 4, 3 bytes, undocumented) is left alone. The udev rule also keeps the camera with its firmware out of USB
autosuspend (power/control on, for 05a9:058c and 058a): found on the owner's board, 2 s after the last program let go
the kernel suspended it, and the camera came back in its dark default exposure (mean luma 2 instead of 113) while the
driver still reported Auto Mode. The window offers Allow access again when it finds a rule from before this. When no
picture comes within 5 s it says so, with the kernel's bandwidth message to look for: Qt reports no error when the
stream cannot start. A PS4 Camera (CUH-ZEY1 or
CUH-ZEY2, which look the same) on Sony's adapter (CFI-ZAA1, found by the adapter's hub 054c:0d0b) is handled the same
way with its own firmware: Download gets the file of PS4 system software 1.50 to 3.15 from psxdev/luke_firmwares,
pinned to a commit and checked by SHA-256 (the file PS Move API tested on a CUH-ZEY2; it has the same video endpoint,
so Reduced bandwidth applies to it too), and the app names the PS4 Camera's four firmware versions that repository
holds. The fix firmware is named when a user picks it but no longer suggested: Auto Mode is what brightens the
picture.
Found on the owner's board (23-10-2026): the camera enumerates in boot mode at 5 Gbit/s on port 7-1, the firmware
loads and the camera comes back as /dev/video1 with 13 modes, but the board's USB 3 controller (AMD FCH xHCI
1022:7814) refuses its only video endpoint (1024 bytes, burst 16, mult 3: 49152 bytes per 125 us), so no mode
streams (VIDIOC_STREAMON: ENOSPC, "Not enough bandwidth for altsetting 1"). USB bandwidth, Reduced by default, sends
the firmware with 2 bursts per interval instead (32768 bytes; 2 bytes of the endpoint's SuperSpeed companion in the
copy that is sent, found by the descriptor, so the fix firmware gets it too); Full sends it as it is. With Reduced
the owner's board streams every one-camera mode at its full rate, 2560x800 at 30 and 15 fps, 1920x520 at 60,
1280x376 at 120, 640x184 at 240 and 3840x1080 at 8; 2560x800 at 60, 3840x1080 at 15 and 30 and 5148x1088 send no
frame. The picture is sound: the preview at 1920x1080 showed the screen the camera was pointed at (the owner
confirmed it, 23-10-2026); a first frame taken as the stream started was mostly dark, before the exposure settled.
